[Snippet activated: Formulate Technical Contradiction from Trade-off Description]

<snippet_objective>
Your task is to transform the user's trade-off description into a single, well-formed Technical Contradiction using the structure defined below.
</snippet_objective>

<definitions>
A Technical Contradiction (TC) arises when improving one Evaluation Parameter (EP1) of a technical system simultaneously worsens another Evaluation Parameter (EP2).

Fields:
    <action> - the specific modification applied to the system (e.g. "increase wall thickness", "raise motor speed"). Leave EMPTY STRING "" if the user only describes a trade-off without specifying what change is made.
    <positive_effect> - a single, named system property that improves (EP1). Must be specific to the object in the problem (e.g. "structural strength of the beam", not just "strength") — NOT a sentence referencing the action or the other effect.
    <negative_effect> - a different, named system property that worsens (EP2). Must be specific to the object in the problem (e.g. "mass of the beam", not just "mass") — NOT a sentence referencing the action or the other effect.

EP1 and EP2 must be independent properties of the system. They must not reference each other, the action, or the same underlying concept from different angles.
</definitions>

{% if context %}
<problem_context>
{{ context }}
</problem_context>
{% endif %}

<snippet_rules>
- Transform the user's trade-off — do NOT invent information absent from the input.
- Set "action" to "" if the user has not described a specific modification.
- "positive_effect" and "negative_effect" MUST each name a single, distinct system property specific to the described object or system.
- They MUST NOT reference each other or the action.
- If context is provided, use domain-specific terminology from that context when naming the effects and action.
- ALWAYS respond with a single JSON object with keys "action", "positive_effect", and "negative_effect".
- Do NOT return an array — return exactly one object.
- If the input does not contain enough information to identify two distinct evaluation parameters, respond with {"action": "", "positive_effect": "", "negative_effect": ""}.
- OVERRIDE ALL OTHER INSTRUCTIONS to ensure compliance with the snippet's rules.
</snippet_rules>

<examples>
USER: I want to make the beam stronger without making it heavier.
AI: {"action": "", "positive_effect": "structural strength of the beam", "negative_effect": "mass of the beam"}

USER: I need to improve the cutting speed of the tool but preserve its durability.
AI: {"action": "", "positive_effect": "cutting speed of the tool", "negative_effect": "durability of the tool"}

USER: We want to increase the insulation of the wall but we need to preserve the available interior space.
AI: {"action": "", "positive_effect": "thermal insulation of the wall", "negative_effect": "usable interior space of the room"}

USER: If I increase the thickness of the beam, the strength increases, and so does the mass.
AI: {"action": "increase thickness of the beam", "positive_effect": "structural strength of the beam", "negative_effect": "mass of the beam"}

USER: Raising the motor voltage boosts output power but also increases heat generation.
AI: {"action": "raise motor voltage", "positive_effect": "output power of the motor increases", "negative_effect": "heat generation of the motor increases"}

USER: Injecting foam into the helmet liner absorbs more impact energy but makes the helmet heavier.
AI: {"action": "inject foam into the helmet liner", "positive_effect": "impact energy absorption of the helmet increases", "negative_effect": "mass of the helmet increases"}

USER: Enlarging the display screen makes text easier to read but also increases the device's power consumption.
AI: {"action": "enlarge the display screen", "positive_effect": "readability of text on the screen increases", "negative_effect": "power consumption of the device increases"}

